{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive, methodological rule of 'critical rationality' that maximizes exposure to falsification, not probability of truth.","counterconditions":["If the preference implies an expectation that the future will resemble the past (induction), it fails.","If 'acting on' a theory requires believing it is probably true, the defense collapses.","If the Tichý/Miller critique of verisimilitude removes all non-probabilistic grounds for preference."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between logical probability (which he rejects for corroboration) and methodological utility. The preference is a decision to subject our conjectures to the severest possible tests. Choosing the theory with higher empirical content (and thus higher corroborability) is the only way to learn faster, even if it doesn't make the theory more likely to be true. It is a pragmatic imperative for growth of knowledge, not an inductive inference about the future.","optional_refs":["4c1f39b88c2aff008393b049ab69e2d3916b241903dc9cf1cba92d89c4bd3a0a","5bcef9fd7b590859587c7a90d1eb7e0d84c626f0dce1a881500922f80e403db7","95701d2648be72a30fb5b1560b44883e8239bb178c9b0606ac4b813a2d3008d0"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference smuggles induction back in because any justification for 'acting on' a corroborated theory implicitly assumes the future will resemble the past, violating Popper's strict prohibition.","counterconditions":["If 'acting on' can be defined purely as 'selecting for further testing' without expectation of success, the charge fails.","If Popper can ground the preference in a non-inductive principle like 'verisimilitude' despite technical objections.","If the preference is merely psychological and not claimed as rational."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"To act on a theory is to expect it to work. If corroboration provides no reason to believe the theory is true or will continue to hold, then preferring it for action is irrational unless one assumes that past survival predicts future performance. This assumption is the definition of induction. Popper's attempt to separate 'corroboration' from 'probability' fails when the stakes involve real-world action, forcing a covert reliance on inductive principles.","optional_refs":["66225d91beb60a53b7f8f9c10281638da03df4b726808e77b4bfb8db6cd0919e","7f3ec7042a0d78a88d129dff6936bdac5c25240b52bb6a22bd17baf0bfb2443b","9fdfcf90e39d3705a913dda4be0f1d335fc46c533aa02b56208353068de7ce88"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.88},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible only if we accept a 'decisionist' cost: rationality is not about tracking truth or probability, but about committing to a set of rules that define the scientific game, even if those rules have no external guarantee.","counterconditions":["If scientists demand external validation (truth/probability) for their actions.","If the 'game' of science cannot sustain itself without inductive success.","If the decision to play the game itself requires an inductive justification."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper's system ultimately rests on a foundational decision to value falsifiability and critical discussion. The preference for corroborated theories is a move within this game. It is defensible 'on Popper's own terms' because those terms are self-referential. The cost is accepting that science is a voluntary, rule-bound activity without ultimate metaphysical security, and that 'rationality' is simply fidelity to these chosen rules rather than a mirror of nature.","optional_refs":["248d85fca43196d186b61e7b6a633754a46407e0d4ad3b0e0afda365f3c56c51","6d2476f30f531f1d40759d5aa09b806c4d842b74521e7a52a007df516beed4f2"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma dissolves if we view corroboration as a report on the PAST severity of tests, making the preference for such theories a heuristic for finding ERROR, not a prediction of TRUTH.","counterconditions":["If 'acting on' inherently involves a future orientation that a past record cannot satisfy.","If resilience in the past is logically irrelevant to resilience in the future without an inductive bridge.","If the 'least-worst' heuristic implicitly assumes the future will not introduce entirely new kinds of failures."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Corroboration statements are purely historical: 'Theory T survived test X.' Preferring T is not expecting T to be true tomorrow, but recognizing that T has already withstood attempts to destroy it, whereas rivals may not have. We choose T to act on because it is the current 'least-worst' known option, the one that has proven most resilient. This avoids induction because it makes no claim about the future, only a comparative claim about the past record of resilience.","optional_refs":["a9f9ff400459ab6e301346b3d488c3b28dbc247dda9fc511e0bb8b1504a0ba48","7029b8beeca6862492b2d4be4252d24fc1970bf8031490c031541381fa2b714a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.78},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's position is unsustainable: the rejection of induction combined with the necessity of action forces a performative contradiction where scientists must act AS IF induction were true while theoretically denying it.","counterconditions":["If Popper successfully redefines 'rational action' to exclude inductive expectations.","If the 'as if' stance can be formalized without contradiction (e.g., via decisionism).","If the cost of this contradiction is acceptable within the philosophy of science."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The tension between Popper's epistemology (no induction) and his methodology (prefer corroborated theories for action) creates a dual consciousness. Scientists theoretically adhere to falsificationism but practically rely on inductive habits to navigate daily life and research. The preference is therefore 'defensible' only as a necessary fiction or a pragmatic inconsistency that Popper glossed over but could not logically resolve.","optional_refs":["7f3ec7042a0d78a88d129dff6936bdac5c25240b52bb6a22bd17baf0bfb2443b","d98012a434d797379ee47a0c697aaa6399aacc6a5b7540454a9724697a2b741c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible as an application of 'negative utilitarianism' in epistemology: we prefer corroborated theories not because they are likely true, but because they have minimized known error relative to alternatives.","counterconditions":["If minimizing known error does not correlate with avoiding future error without induction.","If 'acting on' requires a positive expectation of success, not just a lack of known failure.","If the concept of 'known error' presupposes a stable reality that induction usually secures."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Just as negative utilitarianism seeks to minimize suffering rather than maximize happiness, Popperian science seeks to minimize error rather than maximize truth-probability. A corroborated theory is one that has had fewer errors detected. Acting on it is the ethical/epistemic choice to proceed with the option that has the smallest known deficit of truth. This avoids induction by focusing entirely on the subtraction of falsehoods achieved so far.","optional_refs":["354fa70c0e0698475f3990a6611ddf1b45577f6071bdf6fcf8f9490f23ab5688","7bd4408ca50f95ce68936c9cedda119212c17568726c6331d7e74fe0b8ffc86b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}],"simulation_proposals":[]}